"A constellation is a pattern, not a family. Its stars only look close together because they lie in the same direction. Betelgeuse, Orion's red shoulder, is roughly 550 to 700 light-years away, while Rigel, his bright foot, is about 860 light-years away. The light you see from Betelgeuse tonight left it at least 500 years ago, during the Ming dynasty or even earlier. Stars do drift, but so slowly that the constellations will look the same for your whole life. So step outside tonight and look up: the sky you see belongs to this season.": "f27e60c6e6ac",
"About how much earlier does a star rise each month, and why?": "232c4cb27ff9",
"After the storm, there was a shift in the wind.": "ad2356b86c23",
"Different cultures connected the same stars into different pictures. The constellations astronomers use today come mostly from ancient Greece. Ancient Chinese astronomers divided the sky into the Three Enclosures around the North Pole and the Twenty-Eight Lunar Mansions along the path of the Moon. The two stars in the story of the Cowherd and the Weaver Girl, Altair and Vega, sit on either side of the Milky Way, the \"Silver River.\" They shine high overhead on summer evenings, around the time of the Qixi Festival on the seventh day of the seventh lunar month.": "0f63d027e88d",
"Earth also travels around the Sun, once a year. As it moves along its orbit, the night side points in a slowly changing direction, about one degree a day. In winter, it faces the part of space where Orion is. Six months later, Earth is on the other side of the Sun. Now the night side faces Scorpius, while Orion lies behind the Sun, lost in daylight. The stars have not moved; we have. The constellations along the Sun's yearly path are called the zodiac constellations, and the Sun passes in front of a new one about every month.": "2e5a85e85e95",
"First, the stars never go away. They are in the sky during the day as well, but sunlight scattered by the air makes the daytime sky so bright that it hides them. Night is simply the side of Earth that faces away from the Sun. When your part of the world turns into that side, the glare is gone, and you can finally see out into space. So the stars you see at midnight are the stars in the direction opposite the Sun.": "f90e0f4d12f6",
"Mark the test date on your calendar.": "1a7e1f77a862",
"My little brother can already name three constellations.": "4571a6f3a1c4",
"On a winter evening in Taiwan, Orion stands high in the south, with the three stars of his belt in a neat row. Look for him on a summer evening, and he is gone. In his place, the curling tail of Scorpius hangs low over the southern horizon, with the red star Antares at its heart. About 1,300 years ago, the poet Du Fu wrote that friends who never meet are like Shen and Shang, two groups of stars that are never in the sky together. Shen is the Chinese name for Orion's main stars, and Shang is Antares. Why do they take turns like that?": "9a3066a61060",
"On a winter evening in Taiwan, Orion stands high in the south, with the three stars of his belt in a neat row. Look for him on a summer evening, and he is gone. In his place, the curling tail of Scorpius hangs low over the southern horizon, with the red star Antares at its heart. About 1,300 years ago, the poet Du Fu wrote that friends who never meet are like Shen and Shang, two groups of stars that are never in the sky together. Shen is the Chinese name for Orion's main stars, and Shang is Antares. Why do they take turns like that? First, the stars never go away. They are in the sky during the day as well, but sunlight scattered by the air makes the daytime sky so bright that it hides them. Night is simply the side of Earth that faces away from the Sun. When your part of the world turns into that side, the glare is gone, and you can finally see out into space. So the stars you see at midnight are the stars in the direction opposite the Sun. Earth also travels around the Sun, once a year. As it moves along its orbit, the night side points in a slowly changing direction, about one degree a day. In winter, it faces the part of space where Orion is. Six months later, Earth is on the other side of the Sun. Now the night side faces Scorpius, while Orion lies behind the Sun, lost in daylight. The stars have not moved; we have. The constellations along the Sun's yearly path are called the zodiac constellations, and the Sun passes in front of a new one about every month. You can notice this shift a little every night. A star rises about four minutes earlier each evening. That adds up to about two hours a month, so a star that rises at 11 p.m. this month rises around 9 p.m. next month. After a full year, Earth is back where it started, and the same stars return at the same time of night. That is why farmers in ancient China and Egypt could use the stars as a calendar, telling them when to plant and when to expect the rains or the floods. Different cultures connected the same stars into different pictures. The constellations astronomers use today come mostly from ancient Greece. Ancient Chinese astronomers divided the sky into the Three Enclosures around the North Pole and the Twenty-Eight Lunar Mansions along the path of the Moon. The two stars in the story of the Cowherd and the Weaver Girl, Altair and Vega, sit on either side of the Milky Way, the \"Silver River.\" They shine high overhead on summer evenings, around the time of the Qixi Festival on the seventh day of the seventh lunar month. A constellation is a pattern, not a family. Its stars only look close together because they lie in the same direction. Betelgeuse, Orion's red shoulder, is roughly 550 to 700 light-years away, while Rigel, his bright foot, is about 860 light-years away. The light you see from Betelgeuse tonight left it at least 500 years ago, during the Ming dynasty or even earlier. Stars do drift, but so slowly that the constellations will look the same for your whole life. So step outside tonight and look up: the sky you see belongs to this season.": "a5098906c4cc",
